# Is monopoly always bad?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Can the mere threat of entry discipline a monopolist? When does Baumol's contestable-markets idea hold, and when does it fail?"
next: {"title":"Do monopolies drive innovation?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/35-do-monopolies-drive-innovation.cast"}

## A rival that isn't there
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Before
      - Threat
      - Southwest flies
    values:
      - 100
      - 83
      - 71
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    icons:
      - airplane
      - airplane
      - airplane
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 140
    y_label: Incumbents' fares (before = 100)
    x_label: US routes, 1993–2004
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 28
sources:
    - id: gs2008
      title: How Do Incumbents Respond to the Threat of Entry? Evidence from the Major Airlines
      authors: Austan Goolsbee and Chad Syverson
      year: 2008
      finding: 'On US routes 1993–2004, incumbent fares were about 17% lower once Southwest served both endpoint airports (before it flew the route), 21% lower when it entered, and over 29% lower by the end of the window: over half of Southwest''s effect came before it started flying. Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(4): 1611–1633.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w11072

    text sw "Southwest at both airports" #2f6b8f at.data [2.1,128] font_size 34 hidden true
    text cue17 "−17%" #b5482e at.data [1,106] cites ["gs2008"] font_size 56 hidden true
    text cue29 "−29%" #b5482e at.data [2,102] cites ["gs2008"] font_size 56 hidden true
    text half "over half before it flies" #b5482e at.data [1.5,130] cites ["gs2008"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text door "How many firms?  or  How easy to get in?" at.data [1,132] font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "A rival that isn't there"

Is a monopoly always bad? Start with the fares on an American air route, set to one hundred before anything happens.
    draw axes bar_1

B: Then Southwest, a low-fare airline, starts (@draw sw@) flying from both airports at the ends of that route. Not the route itself, just the two airports.

So the airlines already on the route face no new rival. Not one extra seat.
    highlight bar_1 effect box

B: Your guess: what happens to their fares?
    ask "Drag the Threat bar to where you think fares go." on bar_2 store g default "100"
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true}.

    erase sw

They fall by about seventee (@draw cue17@)n per cent, before Southwest flies a single plane on the route.

B: Once it  (@draw bar_3@)does fly, fares end up about twenty-nine p (@draw cue29@)er cent below where they started.

So more than half of the whole  (@draw half@)fall came from a rival that only might arrive.

B: Austan Goolsbee and Chad Syverson found this on US routes from 1993 to 2004.
    highlight bar_2 bar_3 effect pulse

    erase cue17 cue29 half

The textbook monopolist sells less at a higher price. These airlines seem to be watching the door.
    highlight bar_2 effect circle

B: So maybe what matters isn (@draw door@)'t how many firms there are, but how easy it is to get in.

    pause 0.4

## The threat at the door
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,55]}
vars:
    p:
      value: 30
      min: 12
      max: 45
      step: 1
    prof:
      expr: (p-10)*(100-2*p)-600
sources:
    - id: bpw1982
      title: Contestable Markets and the Theory of Industry Structure
      authors: William J. Baumol, John C. Panzar and Robert D. Willig
      year: 1982
      finding: 'In a perfectly contestable market (entry and exit free, no sunk costs, entrants can come in before incumbents can change price), even a single firm cannot earn more than zero economic profit and prices at average cost; see also Baumol, ''Contestable Markets: An Uprising in the Theory of Industry Structure'', American Economic Review 72(1), 1982.'

    axes axes x_label "Passengers a day (Q)" y_label "Fare ($)" cross corner ticks.x [20,40,60] ticks.y [10,20,30,40,50] hidden true
    curve D #b5482e expr "50 - 0.5*x" x_from 0 x_to 96 hidden true
    label label_D "D" right #b5482e attach_to D hidden true
    curve MC #2f6b8f expr "10 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 96 hidden true
    label label_MC "MC" right #2f6b8f attach_to MC hidden true
    curve ATC #8a5fa8 expr "10 + 600/x" x_from 13.5 x_to 96 hidden true
    label label_ATC "ATC" right #8a5fa8 attach_to ATC hidden true
    dot m #b5482e at.on D at.x 40 hidden true
    curve pm #8f887c expr "30 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 40 dash true hidden true
    text label_pm "$30" #b5482e at.data [45,33] font_size 36 hidden true
    dot c #3d6b3a at.on D at.x 60 hidden true
    curve pc #8f887c expr "20 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 60 dash true hidden true
    text label_pc "$20" #3d6b3a at.data [66,23] font_size 36 hidden true
    curve MR #d0865f expr "50 - x" x_from 0 x_to 42 dash true hidden true
    label label_MR "MR" left #d0865f attach_to MR hidden true
    curve l25 #8f887c expr "25 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 40 hidden true
    region profit x_from 0 x_to 40 between ["pm","l25"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    text label_profit "+$200 a day" at.data [10,27.5] font_size 26 hidden true
    icon hit at.data [72,46] size 80 set twemoji icon_look picture of "motor boat" hidden true
    text label_hit "hit and run" #b5482e at.data [72,39] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "The threat at the door"

To see why a mere threat can bite, take Harbourtown's only ferry to the mainland, and the demand for crossings.
    draw axes D label_D

B: Say, with new illustrative numbers, the boat costs six hundre (@draw MC label_MC@)d dollars a day to run, plus ten dollars for each passenger.

So its average co (@draw ATC label_ATC@)st per passenger, ATC, falls the more people it carries. One boat is all this route can support.

B: As a text (@draw MR label_MR@)book monopolist it sells where MR meets MC: forty passengers a (@draw m pm label_pm@) day at thirty dollars.

That leave (@draw l25 profit label_profit@)s two hundred dollars a day of profit.

B: Now suppose anyone with a boat in  (@draw hit label_hit@)the next harbour could sail in, undercut, and sail away again if it went badly.

    erase profit label_profit l25 MR label_MR

Then thirty dollars is a (@fade m pm label_pm to 0.3@)n open invitation. The only fare that invites nobody is where d (@draw c pc label_pc@)emand meets average cost: twenty dollars, sixty passengers, zero profit.

    erase hit label_hit

B: William Baumol and two colleagues called this a contestable market, in 1982.
    portrait baumol cites ["bpw1982"] x 860 y 540 width 180 of "William Baumol" cameo true

    erase baumol

    dot pt #3d3833 at.on D at.x 40 bind {"at.x":"100 - 2*p"}
    text readout "Fare ${p:0}  ·  profit ${prof:0,} a day" at.data [70,46] font_size 34

Try it: drag the fare above and below twenty dollars, and watch whether a raider could make money.
    explore params ["p"]

B: So a single firm that anyone could challenge prices almost like many. The problem isn't on (@highlight c effect circle@)e firm. It's what keeps the others out.
    animate p 20 duration 2.5

    pause 0.4

## What you can't get back
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,55]}
vars: {"p":{"value":20,"min":10,"max":45}}

    axes axes x_label "Passengers a day (Q)" y_label "Fare ($)" cross corner ticks.x [20,40,60] ticks.y [10,20,30,40,50] hidden true
    curve D #b5482e expr "50 - 0.5*x" x_from 0 x_to 96 hidden true
    label label_D "D" right #b5482e attach_to D hidden true
    curve MC #2f6b8f expr "10 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 96 hidden true
    label label_MC "MC" right #2f6b8f attach_to MC hidden true
    curve ATC #8a5fa8 expr "10 + 600/x" x_from 13.5 x_to 96 hidden true
    label label_ATC "ATC" right #8a5fa8 attach_to ATC hidden true
    dot m #b5482e at.on D at.x 40 hidden true
    curve pm #8f887c expr "30 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 40 dash true hidden true
    text label_pm "$30" #b5482e at.data [45,33] font_size 36 hidden true
    dot c #3d6b3a at.on D at.x 60 hidden true
    curve pc #8f887c expr "20 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 60 dash true hidden true
    text label_pc "$20" #3d6b3a at.data [66,23] font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch cond top-right work ["Hit and run needs:","1. exit gets costs back","2. rival slower to cut"] font_size 28 hidden true
    icon pier at.data [80,47] size 80 set twemoji icon_look picture of bricks hidden true
    text label_pier "new pier: $200,000\nsunk" #b5482e at.data [80,38] font_size 32 hidden true
    dot pt #3d3833 at.on D at.x 60 bind {"at.x":"100 - 2*p"} hidden true
    text readout "Fare ${p:0}" at.data [82,30] font_size 40 hidden true
    text empty "empty threat" #b5482e at.data [80,38] font_size 40 hidden true
    text air1 "plane: mobile" #3d6b3a at.data [78,48] font_size 32 hidden true
    text air2 "gates, slots, hub: sunk" #b5482e at.data [78,42] font_size 32 hidden true
    text air3 "fares: change overnight" #b5482e at.data [78,36] font_size 32 hidden true
    text cant "the part you can't get back" #b5482e at.data [50,52] font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "What you can't get back"

B: Lovely result. But it hangs on the raider leaving for free.
    draw axes D label_D MC label_MC ATC label_ATC m pm label_pm c pc label_pc parallel true

    fade m pm label_pm to 0.3

Hit and run needs two things: what you spend to get (@draw cond_line_2@) in, you get back when you leave; and the incumbent can't  (@draw cond_line_3@)cut its fare faster than you can go.
    draw cond_box cond_line_1

    erase cond

B: Say the raider must first build its own p (@draw pier label_pier@)ier: two hundred thousand dollars of concrete, worth nothing to anyone else. That's a sunk cost.

B: Surely it still comes. Two hundred dollars a day is lying on the table.
    fade m pm label_pm to 1

    quiz "The raider must build a $200,000 pier it can never sell. The ferry charges $30. Does the raider come in?"
        right: No. After the pier is built the ferry can cut its fare and fight, and the raider can't get its money back.
        wrong: Think about what the ferry would do the day after the raider's pier is finished. The answer: no, it stays away.
        * Yes: $200 a day is there
        + No: it would face a fare war it can't escape
        * Only with a bigger boat

    draw pt readout

Picture the day after: two boats on a route that fits one, the fare dragged toward ten dollars, and the pier money gone either way.
    animate p 10 duration 5

    erase pier label_pier

B: The raider sees that coming, so it stays home. And the ferry goes back to thirty dollars.
    animate p 30 duration 2.5

    erase readout

The threat was (@draw empty@) empty. Not because entry costs a lot, but (@highlight m effect circle@) because the cost can't be recovered.

    erase empty

B: Airlines were the standard e (@draw air1@)xample of free entry: a plane can fly a different route tomorrow.

But gate (@draw air2@)s, landing slots and a hub can't move, and  (@draw air3@)fares can change overnight.

    erase air1 air2 air3

B: So it isn't the size of a cost that guards a mon (@draw cant@)opoly. It's the part you can't get back.

    pause 0.4

## How contestable, really?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Before
      - Threat
      - Southwest flies
    values:
      - 100
      - 83
      - 71
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    icons:
      - airplane
      - airplane
      - airplane
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 120
    y_label: Incumbents' fares (before = 100)
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 480
sources:
    - id: gs2008
      title: How Do Incumbents Respond to the Threat of Entry? Evidence from the Major Airlines
      authors: Austan Goolsbee and Chad Syverson
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Incumbent fares about 17% lower once Southwest served both endpoint airports, over 29% lower after it entered the route (US, 1993–2004). Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(4): 1611–1633.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w11072
    - id: hurdle1989
      title: Concentration, Potential Entry, and Performance in the Airline Industry
      authors: Gloria J. Hurdle, Richard L. Johnson, Andrew S. Joskow, Gregory J. Werden and Michael A. Williams
      year: 1989
      finding: 'Fares on 867 nonstop US city pairs in 1985 are best explained by concentration among incumbents together with the number of potential entrants; the hypothesis that all city-pair markets are perfectly contestable is rejected. Journal of Industrial Economics 38(2): 119–139.'
      url: https://ideas.repec.org/a/bla/jindec/v38y1989i2p119-39.html

    text more "−12 more" #b5482e at.data [2,100] cites ["gs2008"] font_size 44 hidden true
    text s1 "Threat disciplines:\nmostly agreed" cites ["hurdle1989","gs2008"] x 820 y 520 font_size 38 hidden true
    text s2 "How often entry\nis that free:\ncontested" #b5482e x 820 y 330 font_size 38 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"\\text{Monopoly: } MR = 50 - Q = 10"},{"tex":"\\Rightarrow Q = 40,\\quad P = \\$30"},{"tex":"\\text{Contestable: } 50 - \\frac{Q}{2} = 10 + \\frac{600}{Q}"},{"tex":"\\Rightarrow Q = 60,\\quad P = \\$20,\\ \\text{profit } 0"}] x 500 y 420 font_size 36 hidden true
    row t1 gap 80 fit.h 200 fit.w 700 fit.x 150 fit.y 430 hidden true
    icon i_truck size 150 set twemoji icon_look picture of "delivery truck" in t1 hidden true
    icon i_train size 150 set twemoji icon_look picture of locomotive in t1 hidden true
    icon i_hosp size 150 set twemoji icon_look picture of hospital in t1 hidden true
    row t2 gap 80 fit.h 200 fit.w 500 fit.x 250 fit.y 430 hidden true
    icon i_plane size 150 set twemoji icon_look picture of airplane in t2 hidden true
    icon i_gate size 150 set twemoji icon_look picture of door in t2 hidden true
    row t3 gap 80 fit.h 200 fit.w 500 fit.x 250 fit.y 430 hidden true
    icon i_house size 150 set twemoji icon_look picture of house in t3 hidden true
    icon i_cable size 150 set twemoji icon_look picture of "electric plug" in t3 hidden true
    scratch close work ["free entry → a lone firm behaves","sunk costs → monopoly bites"] x 500 y 330 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "How contestable, really?"

So how contestable are real markets? Back to the airlines.
    draw axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

B: If the routes were perfectly contestable, the threat alone would do the whole job, and the actual entry would add nothing.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

Instead fares fell a (@draw more@)nother twelve points when Southwest really flew.

B: A 1989 study of 867 routes found the same: would-be entrants lower fares, but the rivals already flying matter too.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 effect pulse

    erase more

So the threat of entry disciplines prices (@draw s1@): mostly agreed. That it does the work of real rivals is rare.

B: The quarrel is over how often entry is  (@draw s2@)that free. Baumol's side saw it in many markets; critics see sunk costs nearly everywhere.

    erase s1 s2 axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the ferry's numbers worked out?

@math

    erase mathq

The monopolist sets MR, fifty minus Q, equal to MC of ten: forty passengers at thirty dollars.
    draw math_box math_line_1 math_line_2

Under contestability the fare equals average cost: fifty minus half of Q equals ten plus six hundred over Q.
    draw math_line_3

That gives sixty passengers at twenty dollars, and zero profit.
    draw math_line_4

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    draw t1 parallel true

    quiz "Which seller is closest to a contestable market?" intro "Three new cases." reveal true reveal_at.x 500 reveal_at.y 330
        right: The food truck: if its prices get fat, a rival truck can drive in, and drive away again with its truck.
        wrong: Ask which seller's costs a newcomer could get back by leaving. Here, the food truck's.
        + A town's only food truck
        * The only railway into a city
        * A region's only hospital

    erase t1

    draw t2 parallel true

    quiz "A small airport's only airline charges high fares. Which change would most likely make it cut them?" reveal true reveal_at.x 500 reveal_at.y 330 voice b
        right: Gates on short leases: entry no longer means sinking money, so the threat becomes real.
        wrong: What makes entering risky for a rival? Here, gates it can rent month by month.
        + Rivals can rent gates month by month
        * It buys a bigger plane
        * A new tax on tickets

    erase t2

    draw t3 parallel true

    quiz "A town's broadband cable is laid in the ground. If a rival laid a second one, what would the incumbent most likely do?" reveal true reveal_at.x 500 reveal_at.y 330
        right: Cut its price: the rival can't dig its cable back up, so a price war hurts it more, and it knows that before it digs.
        wrong: The rival can't take its cable back once it's in the ground. So: cut its price to fight.
        + Cut its price to fight
        * Keep its high price
        * Leave town

    erase t3

So is monopoly always bad? A lone firm that anyone  (@draw close_box close_line_1@)could challenge, and walk away from, behaves itself. What makes monopoly costly is th (@draw close_line_2@)e cost a rival can never get back.

    pause 0.8

